<h2>Why 17 Is the Primary Recruiting Evaluation Year</h2>
<p>Junior year is the single most important recruiting year in high school sports. The bulk of college offers — in basketball, football, volleyball, and track — are made between the fall of junior year and the spring of senior year. Vertical jump numbers recorded at 17 go directly onto recruiting profiles and into scout evaluations. Here is what is happening physically at 17 and why the number carries the most weight it ever will.</p>
<h3>Where 17-Year-Olds Sit in Physical Development</h3>
<p>For most males at 17, testosterone-driven strength and power gains continue through 18 to 19, meaning <strong>meaningful natural development still remains</strong> — but the window is narrower than at 15 or 16. A 17-year-old is building on a mature physical base, so improvements come almost entirely from genuine training adaptation. This makes the quality and consistency of your training the primary driver of vertical jump gains from this point.</p>
<p>For females, pubescent development is complete by 17, so vertical jump gains come entirely from deliberate strength and plyometric training. For a 17-year-old female athlete being actively recruited, this is actually good news: improvement is fully within your control through training, not dependent on waiting for natural growth.</p>
<h3>Why the Junior-Year Number Matters Most</h3>
<p>At 17, coaches are actively making offer decisions. A verified vertical jump number recorded at a reputable combine or camp during junior year is a concrete data point that supports your recruiting case at exactly the moment coaches are deciding. <strong>This is when the number does the most work.</strong> A 17-year-old with a strong, verified vertical has something specific to put in front of a coach during the primary evaluation window; a 17-year-old who has never tested is relying on film and word of mouth at the moment it matters most.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🎯</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Get tested properly, and soon.</strong> If you are 17 and being recruited, get your vertical measured at an official combine, camp, or by a certified trainer during your junior season. Recruiters trust verified numbers from recognized events far more than self-reported figures — and junior year is when that verified number has the most impact on offer decisions. Do not wait until senior year when many offers are already committed.</div>
</div>

<h2>How Much Can a 17 Year Old Improve Their Vertical?</h2>
<p>At 17, real improvement is still available, but it comes almost entirely from deliberate training rather than natural development. For a junior being recruited, a focused off-season can be the difference between an average and a recruiting-level vertical by senior year. Here is what is realistic based on starting point.</p>
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Current Vertical</th>
<th>8-Week Gain (With Training)</th>
<th>12-Month Gain (Consistent)</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Under 15"</td><td>4–6"</td><td>8–12"</td><td>Significant room — strength base usually underdeveloped</td></tr>
<tr class="highlight"><td>15–20"</td><td>3–5"</td><td>6–10"</td><td>Average starting point, strong gains expected</td></tr>
<tr class="highlight"><td>20–25"</td><td>2–4"</td><td>4–8"</td><td>Above average start, solid gains with focused training</td></tr>
<tr><td>25–29"</td><td>1–3"</td><td>3–6"</td><td>Recruiting-level base, marginal gains require precise programming</td></tr>
<tr><td>30"+</td><td>1–2"</td><td>2–4"</td><td>Elite for 17 — protect the number and convert to film</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The biggest factor at any starting point is whether you add <strong>both strength training and plyometrics</strong>. At 17, most athletes have a developed strength base, so plyometrics alone produce diminishing returns unless paired with a serious squat program. Athletes who build real strength alongside jump-specific training see significantly larger gains heading into their senior recruiting year.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">💡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Starting point for strength:</strong> At 17, getting your squat to 1.4 to 1.7 times bodyweight is a realistic, high-impact target that directly supports vertical jump gains. That strength level combined with two structured plyo sessions per week is the most effective program heading into your senior recruiting season. Use our <a href="https://athletecalculator.com/squat-strength-calculator">Squat Strength Predictor</a> to see what your current squat predicts for your vertical.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Best Exercises for 17-Year-Old Athletes</h2>
<p>At 17, athletes have the strength foundation and connective tissue maturity to handle high-intensity plyometric work at full volume. This is a program built to maximize gains in the limited window before senior-year recruiting.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Depth jumps (3 sets of 5, twice per week)</strong> — Stepping off a 12 to 18 inch box and immediately exploding upward on landing. This higher-intensity plyometric is appropriate at 16 and produces some of the fastest reactive strength gains of any exercise.</li>
<li><strong>Squat (3–4 sets of 5–6 at moderate-heavy weight)</strong> — At 16, athletes can train closer to true strength rep ranges. Full depth, controlled descent, progressive overload week to week. This is the foundation that everything else builds on.</li>
<li><strong>Broad jumps (3 sets of 5)</strong> — Continues building horizontal power that transfers to sprint speed and first-step quickness, both of which matter heavily for recruiting evaluations.</li>
<li><strong>Single-leg box jumps (3 sets of 4 per leg)</strong> — Addresses left-right asymmetries that become more relevant as training volume increases, and builds the unilateral power most sports actually use.</li>
<li><strong>Approach jump practice (daily, 5–10 minutes)</strong> — Refining the one-step and two-step approach technique. By 16, technical efficiency in the approach can add 2 to 3 inches on its own with no fitness change.</li>
</ul>
<p>A 17-year-old can typically handle <strong>3 to 4 jump-focused sessions per week</strong> in the off-season, or 2 during the competitive season to manage fatigue. For a junior with one off-season before senior-year recruiting, maximizing off-season volume with adequate recovery is the single most impactful thing you can do for your vertical.</p>

